bash/sh vs redis-cli — வித்தியாசம் என்ன?     ITS IMPORTENT

bash / sh = Operating System shell. இது container-ஓட Linux OS உள்ள போக உதவும் — ls, pwd, cd, file operations போன்ற OS-level commands run பண்ண.

redis-cli = Redis-க்கே specific-ஆன ஒரு application/client program. இது OS shell இல்ல — இது Redis server process-ஓட நேரடியா பேச (
communicate) பண்ண உதவுற ஒரு tool. Redis server ஒரு தன்னுடைய "language" (protocol)-ல தான் commands புரிஞ்சுக்கும்
(SET, GET, PING etc.) — bash-க்கு அது தெரியாது
Container
│
├── Linux OS (bash / sh)
│      │
│      ├── ls
│      ├── pwd
│      ├── cd
│      └── ps
│
└── Redis Server
       │
       ├── redis-cli
       │      ├── PING
       │      ├── SET
       │      ├── GET
       │      └── DEL
nterviewer: Why do we use redis-cli instead of bash to access Redis?

Answer:

bash or sh is a Linux shell used to interact with the container's operating system. redis-cli is a Redis client that communicates with the Redis server using the Redis protocol.
Linux commands (ls, pwd) work in the shell, while Redis commands (PING, SET, GET) work in redis-cli.

This distinction applies to many other applications too:

MySQL → mysql
PostgreSQL → psql
MongoDB → mongosh
Redis → redis-cli

In each case, you first enter the container with kubectl exec, then use the application's own client to communicate with that application.

