5 Interview Golden Answer
------------------------
"Kubernetes revision history is append-only, like a git log. When you run rollout undo, 
Kubernetes doesn't restore the old revision number — it creates a brand-new revision that copies the target's pod template. So after a rollback, 
you'll see the revision counter keep incrementing forward, never going backward. This design keeps a clean, 
chronological audit trail of every change, including rollbacks."

